<script module>
  import Demo from "$ui/components/Demo.abide"
  import MemoReuseDemo from "$ui/demos/caching/MemoReuseDemo.abide"
  import ServerStateDemo from "$ui/demos/caching/ServerStateDemo.abide"
</script>

<main class="page">
  <h1>The memo primitive</h1>
  <p class="lead"><code>memo(fn)</code> wraps ANY async function into a memoized slot: cache, in-flight coalescing, and a reactive read surface (<code>peek</code>/<code>pending</code>/<code>refreshing</code>/<code>error</code>/<code>watch</code> + <code>refresh</code>/<code>invalidate</code>/<code>publish</code>). <strong>RPC and sockets are memo + a transport</strong> — the same slot machinery, reached the same way on both sides. Here it runs entirely in the browser over a plain async function.</p>

  <p>The handler counts how many times it actually ran. Load once, then read again and again — the count <strong>holds</strong> because the slot is reused (and concurrent reads coalesce onto one run). <code>invalidate</code> drops the slot, so the next read re-runs and the count <strong>climbs</strong>.</p>
  <Demo id="memo-reuse" title="<code>memo(fn)</code> — a memoized slot over a plain promise" client="src/ui/demos/caching/MemoReuseDemo.abide" tab="client">
    <MemoReuseDemo />
  </Demo>

  <h2><code>state</code> is isomorphic too — a server-side reactive graph</h2>
  <p>The same reasoning applies to <a href="/templating/reactivity"><code>state</code></a>. It's the <em>sync, owned</em> face of the same atom <code>memo</code> is built on, and it lives in <code>abide/shared/*</code> — so it isn't client-only. A <strong>server</strong> module can own a <code>state</code>, and other server modules import it, derive from it (<code>state.computed</code>), and subscribe to it (<code>watch</code>): a reactive graph running entirely on the server. Here two RPCs (separate modules) write and read a <code>state</code> in <code>$shared/serverReactive</code>; the write propagates through a server-side <code>computed</code> and fires a server-side <code>watch</code>.</p>
  <p>One thing stands out in the server tab: the explicit <code>total()</code> and <code>total.set(x)</code>. Inside a <code>.abide</code> <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> you write bare <code>total</code> and <code>total = x</code>, and the compiler rewrites them to those calls for you. There's no compiler in plain <code>.ts</code>, so you call the <code>State</code> surface directly — a cell is <strong>callable</strong>: <code>total()</code> (tracked read, subscribes the enclosing <code>computed</code>/<code>watch</code>), <code>.set(next)</code> (publish), and <code>.peek()</code> (untracked read). It's the same cell either way; the bare-identifier sugar is a <code>.abide</code>-only convenience.</p>
  <Demo id="server-state" title="<code>state</code> + <code>computed</code> + <code>watch</code> across server modules" server="src/shared/serverReactive.ts" client="src/ui/demos/caching/ServerStateDemo.abide" tab="server">
    <ServerStateDemo />
  </Demo>
  <p class="note">Module-level server <code>state</code> is <strong>process-global</strong> (one value across all requests) — right for a global like this counter; for mutable per-request or per-user state, reach for <code>memo: {'{'} shared {'}'}</code> instead, which is guarded and identity-safe.</p>
</main>
